//
// A fn-ref whose `??` may hand back its ARGUMENT must not have that argument's store adopted
// by the caller, and the caller can only refuse the adoption if it can NAME the store
// (loft#1318, `formal/ownership.md` @FR-O-Oracle).
//
// `(O-Oracle)` says a call resolves through the callee's return summary, and a `Join` is
// decided at run time by store identity against the base the dep names.  Everything here is
// about that base being FINDABLE: where the oracle could not name one it answered `Owned` —
// the one verdict that licenses a free — so the caller released a container it still held, and
// the next read came back `null` with nothing said.
//
// Three separate facts were missing, and each has a cell below:
//
//   * an argument that is itself a CALL is resolved by the oracle, not by the structural
//     walk, so `g(pick(vs, 0))` roots at `vs` one frame further out — and so does `g(vs[0])`,
//     whose nullable element read the structural walk does not carry either;
//   * the oracle's own projection set has to carry every store-preserving read, or a keyed
//     lookup (`m[k].v`) reads as a mint and the caller loses its own collection;
//   * a hidden `__retbuf` IS nameable — the caller allocates the buffer and passes it — so a
//     callee delivering through one hands back a store the caller already holds.
//
// ⚠ THE MINT ARM IS THE OTHER HALF, AND IT IS WHAT SEPARATES THIS FROM A DECLINE.  Refusing to
// lift also stops the over-free, and costs one leaked store per call.  The ceiling cell is
// what says a witness was named instead: 70 000 calls through a call-shaped argument cannot
// complete on a build that holds a store per call.
//
// ⚠ AND `g(mint())` MUST STILL ADOPT.  A callee that MINTS gives the caller a store no caller
// variable holds, so the base stays unnameable there ON PURPOSE and the free is right.  That
// cell is the control for the direction of the whole change.
//
// ⚠ THE STATEMENT CONTEXT IS AN AXIS, AND TWO CELLS HERE WERE VACUOUS BEFORE IT WAS MEASURED.
// `s += g(vs[0])[1]` and `a = g(pick(vs, 0))[1]` are BOTH correct on the broken build — the
// accumulate and bind paths reach a different lift decision — while the same reads inside an
// interpolation are wrong.  So the projection and straight-line cells are written in the
// interpolated form on purpose; rewriting either into the arithmetic form silently retires it.
// The call-shaped argument breaks in either context, which is why its own cell can accumulate.
//
// ⚠ AND ONE TEST FUNCTION PER CONTAINER KIND, not one holding all four.  A cell that asserts
// on the hash before it reaches the index reports the index as unmoved whatever the index
// does — and the index and sorted kinds FAULT on the broken build where the hash answers
// quietly wrong, so they are the two rows that would have been lost.
//
// @falsified-at: b1bd3212 — interpret exit 1 -> 0, interpret 6 assertion failures -> 0, native
//   exit 1 -> 0, native 1 assertion failures -> 0.
// ⚠ THE COUNTS UNDERSTATE THE FILE, AND KNOWING BY HOW MUCH IS THE POINT.  Eight of the
//   fourteen cells fail on that build; the row says six because the `sorted` and `index` cells
//   fail with `Store access out of bounds`, which the runner reports as a failed test rather
//   than an `assertion failed:` line, and which never reaches the PANIC channel either because
//   the runner catches it.  Native reads 1 because it stops at its first failing test
//   function — a floor, not a census.  The six cells that PASS on that build are the controls:
//   field spelling, minting argument, the ceiling, the branch arm, the capture and the named
//   spelling.
//   HOLDS: leak, panic, free-refusal, expectations are equal on both trees. Falsify prints
//   only the channels that MOVE and requires HERE to be clean, so each of those is clean on
//   both. The native side was not scored in this sweep.

struct Rk1318 { k: integer, v: vector<integer> }
struct Bx1318 { rows: vector<integer> }

fn pick1318(v: vector<vector<integer>>, i: integer) -> vector<integer>? { v[i] }
fn hash1318(m: hash<Rk1318[k]>, key: integer) -> vector<integer>? { m[key].v }
fn sorted1318(m: sorted<Rk1318[k]>, key: integer) -> vector<integer>? { m[key].v }
fn index1318(m: index<Rk1318[k]>, key: integer) -> vector<integer>? { m[key].v }
fn copy1318(v: vector<vector<integer>>) -> vector<vector<integer>> { v }
fn mint1318() -> vector<integer>? { [41, 42] }
fn dflt1318(q: vector<integer>?) -> vector<integer> { q ?? [7, 8] }

fn test_a_call_shaped_argument_leaves_the_callers_vector_intact() {
  // The filed shape.  The `??` hands `q` straight back, and `q` is a view into `vs`, so a
  // caller adopting it releases `vs` — the second iteration then reads out of bounds.
  vs: vector<vector<integer>> = [[41, 42], [51, 52]];
  g = fn(q: vector<integer>?) -> vector<integer> { q ?? [7, 8] };
  s = 0;
  for _ in 0..5 { s += g(pick1318(vs, 0))[1]; }
  assert(s == 210, "call-shaped argument sum: got {s}");
  assert(len(vs) == 2, "the source vector's length after five borrows: got {len(vs)}");
  assert(vs[0][0] == 41, "the source's first element: got {vs[0][0]}");
}

fn test_a_hash_lookup_argument_roots_at_the_hash() {
  // A keyed lookup is a store-preserving read like an element read; a set short by it made the
  // oracle call `m[key].v` a mint.  The hash answers quietly wrong on the broken build.
  m: hash<Rk1318[k]> = [Rk1318 { k: 1, v: [41, 42] }];
  g = fn(q: vector<integer>?) -> vector<integer> { q ?? [7, 8] };
  s = 0;
  for _ in 0..5 { s += g(hash1318(m, 1))[1]; }
  assert(s == 210, "hash-rooted sum: got {s}");
  assert(len(m) == 1, "the hash's length after five borrows: got {len(m)}");
}

fn test_a_sorted_lookup_argument_roots_at_the_collection() {
  // The same read at the sorted kind, which FAULTS on the broken build rather than answering
  // wrong — a different channel, and the reason each kind is its own test function.
  t: sorted<Rk1318[k]> = [Rk1318 { k: 1, v: [41, 42] }];
  g = fn(q: vector<integer>?) -> vector<integer> { q ?? [7, 8] };
  s = 0;
  for _ in 0..5 { s += g(sorted1318(t, 1))[1]; }
  assert(s == 210, "sorted-rooted sum: got {s}");
  assert(len(t) == 1, "the sorted collection's length after five borrows: got {len(t)}");
}

fn test_an_index_lookup_argument_roots_at_the_collection() {
  // And at the index kind, which faults the same way.
  x: index<Rk1318[k]> = [Rk1318 { k: 1, v: [41, 42] }];
  g = fn(q: vector<integer>?) -> vector<integer> { q ?? [7, 8] };
  s = 0;
  for _ in 0..5 { s += g(index1318(x, 1))[1]; }
  assert(s == 210, "index-rooted sum: got {s}");
  assert(len(x) == 1, "the index's length after five borrows: got {len(x)}");
}

fn test_a_nullable_element_read_reaches_the_oracle() {
  // `g(vs[0])` reaches the call as `OpGetVectorNullable`, a spelling the STRUCTURAL root walk
  // does not carry — so this cell is closed by the oracle route, not by naming the op.  Adding
  // the two nullable spellings to `is_projection_op` also closes it and is blocked for an
  // unrelated reason; `use_analysis::projection_ops` carries that measurement.
  //
  // Interpolated on purpose: the arithmetic form of this same read is correct on the broken
  // build and would score nothing.
  vs: vector<vector<integer>> = [[41, 42], [51, 52]];
  g = fn(q: vector<integer>?) -> vector<integer> { q ?? [7, 8] };
  lens = 0;
  last = "";
  for _ in 0..5 { last = "{g(vs[0])[1]}"; lens += len(vs); }
  assert(last == "42", "the last interpolated element: got {last}");
  assert(lens == 10, "the source's length summed over five borrows: got {lens}");
}

fn test_a_field_read_argument_is_the_control_for_the_spelling_axis() {
  // `OpGetField` was on both lists throughout, so this spelling was always right.  It is here
  // as the SIBLING of the two that were not: a change fixing the nullable element read by
  // widening something too far would show up as this cell moving.
  b = Bx1318 { rows: [41, 42] };
  g = fn(q: vector<integer>?) -> vector<integer> { q ?? [7, 8] };
  lens = 0;
  last = "";
  for _ in 0..5 { last = "{g(b.rows)[1]}"; lens += len(b.rows); }
  assert(last == "42", "the last interpolated field element: got {last}");
  assert(lens == 10, "the field's length summed over five borrows: got {lens}");
}

fn test_a_delivery_buffer_is_a_nameable_base() {
  // `copy1318` returns through the caller's own `__ref_N` buffer, so the view handed back
  // lives in a store the caller holds — under a name no argument carries.
  vs: vector<vector<integer>> = [[41, 42], [51, 52]];
  g = fn(q: vector<integer>?) -> vector<integer> { q ?? [7, 8] };
  s = 0;
  for _ in 0..5 { s += g(pick1318(copy1318(vs), 0))[1]; }
  assert(s == 210, "buffer-rooted sum: got {s}");
  assert(len(vs) == 2, "the source vector's length: got {len(vs)}");
}

fn test_a_minting_argument_is_still_adopted() {
  // The control for the direction: `mint1318()` hands over a store no caller variable names,
  // so the lift and its free are correct.  A change answering "borrowed" everywhere would turn
  // this cell into 70 000 leaked stores and abort at the ceiling.
  g = fn(q: vector<integer>?) -> vector<integer> { q ?? [7, 8] };
  s = 0;
  for _ in 0..70000 { s += g(mint1318())[1]; }
  assert(s == 2940000, "minting-argument sum: got {s}");
}

fn test_the_mint_arm_still_costs_no_store_per_call() {
  // 70 000 > the 65535-store ceiling: a build that declines the lift instead of naming a
  // witness cannot reach the assert.  The ceiling IS the assertion.
  empty: vector<vector<integer>> = [];
  g = fn(q: vector<integer>?) -> vector<integer> { q ?? [7, 8] };
  s = 0;
  for _ in 0..70000 { s += g(pick1318(empty, 0))[1]; }
  assert(s == 560000, "mint-arm sum: got {s}");
}

fn test_both_arms_alternate_over_one_call_site() {
  // The two arms through one site: the borrow arm must leave `vs` whole and the mint arm must
  // not accumulate, which no single-arm cell can say together.
  vs: vector<vector<integer>> = [[41, 42], [51, 52]];
  g = fn(q: vector<integer>?) -> vector<integer> { q ?? [7, 8] };
  s = 0;
  for i in 0..400 { s += g(pick1318(vs, if i % 2 == 0 { 0 } else { 9 }))[1]; }
  assert(s == 10000, "alternating sum: got {s}");
  assert(len(vs) == 2, "the source vector's length: got {len(vs)}");
}

fn test_a_loop_is_not_required() {
  // Two straight-line calls fail too — the free lands at the end of the first statement, and
  // the loop only makes it easy to see.  Interpolated for the reason the projection cell is.
  vs: vector<vector<integer>> = [[41, 42], [51, 52]];
  g = fn(q: vector<integer>?) -> vector<integer> { q ?? [7, 8] };
  a = "{g(pick1318(vs, 0))[1]}";
  b = "{g(pick1318(vs, 0))[1]}";
  assert(a == "42", "the first straight-line call: got {a}");
  assert(b == "42", "the second straight-line call: got {b}");
  assert(len(vs) == 2, "the source vector's length: got {len(vs)}");
}

fn test_a_branch_arm_context_is_unmoved() {
  // A call in an `if` arm reaches a different lift decision than one in an argument position,
  // and the collection `??` return leaks there for a reason of its own (`formal/closures.md`
  // D-clo-14's residual).  With a call-shaped argument it is clean on both counts, and saying
  // so keeps the two mechanisms apart when the next one is worked.
  vs: vector<vector<integer>> = [[41, 42], [51, 52]];
  g = fn(q: vector<integer>?) -> vector<integer> { q ?? [7, 8] };
  s = 0;
  for i in 0..400 { r = if i % 2 == 0 { g(pick1318(vs, 0)) } else { g(pick1318(vs, 9)) }; s += r[1]; }
  assert(s == 10000, "branch-arm sum: got {s}");
  assert(len(vs) == 2, "the source vector's length: got {len(vs)}");
}

fn test_a_captured_default_arm_is_unmoved() {
  // The default arm hands back a CAPTURE rather than a fresh mint, which reaches the return
  // through `__closure` and has no argument witness at all — a separate route
  // (`formal/closures.md` D-clo-7).  Both arms stay correct and neither container is released,
  // which says naming the argument's base did not disturb the capture's.
  vs: vector<vector<integer>> = [[41, 42], [51, 52]];
  cap: vector<integer> = [90, 91];
  g = fn(q: vector<integer>?) -> vector<integer> { q ?? cap };
  s = 0;
  for _ in 0..5 { s += g(pick1318(vs, 0))[1]; }
  t = 0;
  for _ in 0..5 { t += g(pick1318(vs, 9))[1]; }
  assert(s == 210, "captured-default borrow sum: got {s}");
  assert(t == 455, "captured-default capture sum: got {t}");
  assert(len(vs) == 2, "the source vector's length: got {len(vs)}");
  assert(len(cap) == 2, "the capture's length: got {len(cap)}");
}

fn test_the_named_spelling_is_unchanged() {
  // A direct call never degraded to `Owned`, so this spelling was correct throughout: the
  // control that says the change did not reach it.
  vs: vector<vector<integer>> = [[41, 42], [51, 52]];
  s = 0;
  for _ in 0..5 { s += dflt1318(pick1318(vs, 0))[1]; }
  assert(s == 210, "named-spelling sum: got {s}");
  assert(len(vs) == 2, "the source vector's length: got {len(vs)}");
}
